{
  "metadata": {
    "name": "PiCrawler \u2014 Cruse v2 baseline (Phase 7.13, pre-bucket \u2014 canonical pre-bucket reference for the gait-bucket bet A/Bs)",
    "description": "File: the_picrawler_stand_target_per_servo_perceptual_cpg_trot_cruse_v2.json\nChat refs: BASE / cruse_v2 baseline (pre-bucket; canonical comparison point for the bucket bet A/Bs)\n\nPhase 7.5 perceptual-CPG variant G2: trot gait pattern (FL+RR in phase=0, FR+RL in phase=\u03c0) at period_ticks=60 (~1.0 sim sec).  Most common quadruped running gait \u2014 diagonal pairs lift together for balance.  Tests whether the gait family encoded in the phase relationships matters or only the rhythm itself.\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\n  \u2022 Same standing baseline as G1.\n  \u2022 Different gait structure if brain latches on: FL+RR should move together, FR+RL together (diagonal-pair coordination).\n  \u2022 Compare body translation to G1 (lateral-seq) \u2014 if trot is better suited to picrawler's mass distribution, this could outperform.\n  \u2022 Fall rate comparable to G1.\n\nPhase 7.5.R+ \u2014 adds epm_radial_compass clustering the 2-D body-frame outward-direction vector (reality.proprio.radial_compass).  Diagnosed gap from G2_trot+gated UI observation: brain found a gait but motion was directionally random (median |v|=0.05 m/s; only 7 mm/s net outward; only 1/6 bursts efficiently outward).  Radial-compass gives egocentric goal vector so brain has a sensor for \"which way is outward\" instead of learning navigation purely from delayed reward.\n\nPhase 7.8 variant: predictive value head on all 12 Premotors (value_head_gain=1.0, lr=0.01, lookahead=30 ticks \u2248 0.5s).  Each Premotor maintains V[intent, latent] that predicts accumulated reward in the next lookahead window if that intent is chosen.  Action selection biases logits forward-looking: intents with high predicted future reward get more probability.\n\nDiagnosed gap: REINFORCE is backward-looking (credit-assign past); picrawler needs forward-looking bias.  V head learns this by training against actual reward observed in the lookahead window, then biases softmax at action selection time.  Uses EPM's prediction principle applied to action-conditioned reward.\n\nPhase 7.9 variant SV: SynergyTimer + ValueHead composed.  Both pre-softmax additive biases.  Tests whether the two complementary mechanisms (stability lift from value head + navigation timing from synergy timer) compose constructively.\n\nPhase 7.10 variant: adds adaptive entropy anneal on top of D (synergy+value).  entropy_anneal_gain=0.5, threshold=1.2, alpha=0.005 (~3s window).  Each Premotor tracks entropy_ema; when it stays above 1.2 (near uniform for n=5 intents where max H = log(5) \u2248 1.61), softmax T is multiplied DOWN by up to 0.5\u00d7 to sharpen the distribution and force commitment.  Addresses the rear-leg dropout loop diagnosed in seed-51 long run: rear Premotors had H=1.38-1.42 and W=6 (vs front H=0.58-0.65 W=10), producing diffuse policies with weighted_accel \u2248 0 (legs visually stiff).  Anneal forces commitment so the brain experiences non-zero rear-leg accel \u2192 REINFORCE gets discriminative gradient \u2192 feedback loop broken.  Self-correcting: once entropy drops, T returns to baseline.\n\nPhase 7.11 variant: replaces SynergyTimer with CruseCoordinator on top of the anneal config.  Cruse Rule 1 (no-swing-overlap): when a rear leg's anatomical anterior is in swing, the rear leg's hip2 + knee Premotors get pre-softmax bias toward stance-direction intents.  Adaptive magnitude \u2014 bias fades when brain is naturally compliant.\n\nAnatomical wiring: RL follows FL, RR follows FR.  Front legs (FL, FR) have no anterior and get zero bias.  Hip1 Premotors get zero bias (yaw control, no stance/swing axis).\n\nTests whether structural inter-leg coordination prior (the missing piece per the 7.5-7.10 substrate ceiling diagnosis) lifts navigation.\n\nPhase 7.13 \u2014 Hector-faithful CruseCoordinator (v2).  Direct response to Phase 7.11 v1 falsification.  The v1 implementation was 1/6 of Walknet (Rule 1 only, soft logit-bias authority, adaptive_magnitude that faded when brain seemed compliant).  Observed rear-leg stiffness in launcher matched the diagnosis: Rule 1 alone is PREVENTATIVE (don't lift) without constructive partner (when to lift).\n\nv2 changes:\n  Rule 1 (no-swing-overlap, preventative): anterior in swing \u2192 STANCE bias\n  Rule 2 (release, CONSTRUCTIVE NEW): anterior just-touchdown \u2192 SWING bias.\n      Fires within rule2_window_ticks (15) of anterior touchdown.  Triggers\n      the lift that Rule 1 alone never causes.\n  Rule 3 (contralateral load tolerance, NEW): contralateral in swing \u2192\n      strengthen own STANCE.  Half-weight relative to Rule 1.\n  adaptive_magnitude=false: rules fire at CONSTANT gain when active.\n      Hector's rules are invariants; they don't fade.  Set to true to\n      reproduce v1 behavior.\n  rule1_violation_boost=1.5: Rule 1 contribution \u00d7 1.5 when both active\n      AND currently violated.  Sharper authority on tip-over risk.\n\nContralateral wiring (FL\u2194FR, RL\u2194RR) added for Rule 3.\nTelemetry: total_rule1/2/3_fires counters in snapshot_state.",
